  {
    question: 'What is the official definition of a dwarf planet?',
    answer: 'The International Astronomical Union (IAU) defined a dwarf planet in 2006 as a body that: (1) orbits the Sun, (2) has sufficient mass for its self-gravity to overcome rigid body forces so that it assumes a hydrostatic equilibrium (roughly round) shape, (3) has NOT cleared the neighbourhood around its orbit, and (4) is not a satellite. Planets meet all four criteria; dwarf planets fail criterion 3. Pluto, Eris, Haumea, Makemake, and Ceres are the five officially recognized dwarf planets.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Why was Pluto reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006?',
    answer: "Pluto was reclassified because it shares its orbital neighborhood with thousands of other Kuiper Belt Objects and has not gravitationally cleared that region. The discovery of Eris in 2005 — a Kuiper Belt Object slightly more massive than Pluto — forced astronomers to confront the question: if Pluto is a planet, so is Eris, and so are dozens of other similar objects. Rather than expanding the planet count, the IAU created the dwarf planet category to handle these bodies scientifically."
  },
